WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.772
Prosecution Framework, Phase 6, Legal Escalation.

00:00:03.772 --> 00:00:14.375
This page presents two parallel legal instruments: domestic private prosecution under Section 504 of the Criminal Code, and international criminal referral under Rome Statute Article 7.

00:00:14.375 --> 00:00:20.012
Both are designed to bypass the captured policing chain and establish accountability through the courts.

00:00:20.012 --> 00:00:29.068
The applicable Criminal Code sections include: Section 504 for private prosecution, allowing any person to lay an Information before a Justice of the Peace.

00:00:29.068 --> 00:00:32.113
Section 46 for high treason and treason.

00:00:32.113 --> 00:00:37.749
Section 380 for fraud over 5,000 dollars, covering ArriveCAN and Phoenix Pay.

00:00:37.749 --> 00:00:41.374
Section 122 for breach of trust by public officer.

00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:44.159
Section 219 for criminal negligence.

00:00:44.159 --> 00:00:49.420
And Section 241 for counselling or aiding suicide, applicable to MAID cases.

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:53.227
Form 1: Private Prosecution under Section 504.

00:00:53.227 --> 00:00:58.136
Any person may lay an Information before a Justice of the Peace in any province.

00:00:58.136 --> 00:01:05.965
The Universal Venue Doctrine argues that for offences committed across all federal domains, jurisdiction is arguable in any provincial court.

00:01:05.965 --> 00:01:12.227
The Information form includes the names of the accused, the dates, locations, and Criminal Code sections violated.

00:01:12.227 --> 00:01:14.876
How to file a private prosecution in Canada.

00:01:14.876 --> 00:01:19.727
Five steps: First, research the specific offence using evidence dossiers.

00:01:19.727 --> 00:01:23.374
Second, prepare the Information form with specific facts.

00:01:23.374 --> 00:01:28.579
Third, attend before a Justice of the Peace at your local courthouse to swear the Information.

00:01:28.579 --> 00:01:33.227
Fourth, a pre-enquiry hearing under Section 507.1.

00:01:33.227 --> 00:01:41.840
Fifth, if the Crown stays the prosecution under Section 579, document it as evidence of institutional capture for your ICC referral.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.818
Form 2: International Criminal Court referral under Rome Statute Article 7.

00:01:46.818 --> 00:01:57.579
When the domestic system proves unwilling or unable to prosecute under the complementarity principle of Article 17, a Communication may be submitted to the Office of the Prosecutor at The Hague.

00:01:57.579 --> 00:02:04.420
If a Section 504 prosecution is stayed by the Crown, that stay itself constitutes evidence of unwillingness.

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:06.511
The Institutional Malice Pattern.

00:02:06.511 --> 00:02:08.705
These are not six separate scandals.

00:02:08.705 --> 00:02:18.988
They are six facets of a single institutional pattern: systematic extraction of public resources, erosion of citizen rights, and elimination of accountability mechanisms.

00:02:18.988 --> 00:02:29.159
The domestic prosecution establishes the legal record, and when the Crown inevitably stays it, that stay becomes the evidence of unwillingness required for ICC complementarity.
